        id: "se-contract-no-codification",
        title: "No comprehensive civil code — the Nordic legal family",
        explanation:
          "Start with the structural fact that shapes literally everything else you will hear in this lecture. Sweden's contract law is governed primarily by the 1915 Avtalslagen, the Contracts Act, together with separate, purpose-built statutes rather than one unifying instrument: the Köplagen, the 1990 Sale of Goods Act, for commercial sales between businesses, the Konsumentköplagen, the Consumer Sales Act, for consumer transactions, and further dedicated statutes covering specific areas such as real property transfers under the Jordabalken, the Land Code. Sweden never adopted anything resembling Germany's 1900 BGB or France's 1804 Code civil, a single, systematically organized text a lawyer can simply open to the relevant article for nearly any private-law question. Instead, Swedish law belongs to the Nordic legal family, a genuinely distinct branch of the civil-law tradition that developed through close nineteenth- and twentieth-century Scandinavian legislative cooperation among Sweden, Denmark, Norway, and Finland, producing broadly harmonized, but formally separate, national statutes on shared subjects like contract formation and sale of goods, rather than one shared regional code. Add to this a strong Swedish tradition of förarbeten, the legislature's own detailed preparatory works and explanatory reports accompanying each statute, which Swedish courts treat as genuinely authoritative interpretive material, considerably more central to everyday legal reasoning than the travaux préparatoires a French court might occasionally consult, and you have a system where the statutory text is only the starting point of the analysis, never its end.",
