        title: "Löftesprincipen — the binding-offer principle",
        explanation:
          "Now, turn to how a contract actually forms under this distributed statutory framework. Under Chapter 1 of the Avtalslagen, an offer, anbud, is traditionally treated as binding on the offeror once made, the löftesprincipen, or promise principle: the offeror generally cannot freely revoke it before the recipient responds, unless the offer explicitly reserves that right or the offer's own terms otherwise indicate it is not meant to bind. The offeree's acceptance, accept, must then match the offer's terms; a purported acceptance that actually changes those terms operates instead as a new counter-offer, oren accept, reversing the parties' roles rather than concluding the contract on the original terms. This contrasts sharply with common law, where an offer is generally freely revocable at any time before acceptance, and it differs too from the more negotiation-focused, less formally binary approach some civil-law systems take to the offer-acceptance sequence, since Swedish doctrine still analyzes formation through this same fairly mechanical anbud-accept structure even though the underlying philosophy, binding the offeror to their own word, is distinctly Scandinavian in origin. The Avtalslagen also fixes an acceptfrist under §§ 1 through 3, an implied or expressly stated deadline within which the acceptance must actually reach the offeror, and § 4 treats an acceptance that arrives after that window has closed as a fresh offer of its own, not as a valid acceptance of the original terms.",
