          "Now, turn to how a contract that formed validly, met whatever form it required, and was concluded by a properly authorized party can still unravel. Chapter 3 of the Avtalslagen sets out specific ogiltighetsgrunder, grounds of invalidity: § 28 covers tvång, duress, distinguishing genuinely coercive threats from ordinary hard bargaining, § 29 addresses a milder form of duress that still permits some limited enforcement, § 30 covers svek, fraud, a deliberate deception used to induce the agreement, and § 31 covers ocker, usury, exploiting another party's distress, inexperience, or dependent position to extract grossly disproportionate terms. Section 32 addresses mistake, misstag, in how a declaration was actually transmitted or understood, a narrower doctrine than the broader mistake concepts some civil-law systems recognize. Section 33, considerably broader than any of these specific grounds, then adds a general clause allowing a court to invalidate an agreement whose enforcement would be contrary to tro och heder, good faith and honor, where the party seeking to enforce it knew of circumstances at formation that made enforcement genuinely dishonorable, even where none of the narrower, named grounds technically apply. Each ground carries its own remedy consequence: a successful finding under §§ 28 through 32 generally voids the agreement retroactively as though it never bound the invalidating party at all, while § 33 gives the court somewhat more flexibility to invalidate only the specific offending part of an otherwise sound agreement.",
