          "Now, turn to what happens once a validly formed, unimpeachable contract actually gets breached. Sweden's 1990 Köplagen was drafted with substantial influence from the United Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods, the CISG, which Sweden ratified, giving Swedish commercial sales law a remedy structure that aligns more closely with international commercial law conventions than a purely domestically developed framework might: rättelse, cure, letting the seller correct a defective delivery within a reasonable time, hävning, avoidance or termination, available only for a väsentligt avtalsbrott, a fundamental breach, prisavdrag, a proportionate price reduction reflecting the defect's actual impact on value, and skadestånd, damages, calculated broadly on an expectation basis that lets the buyer recover the loss actually flowing from the breach. The Köplagen also imports the CISG's own reklamation, notice-of-defect, discipline: a buyer who fails to notify the seller of a defect within a reasonable time after discovering it, and in any event within two years, generally loses the right to rely on it at all [4]. The statute also sets a clear default rule on när risken går över, when risk of loss actually passes to the buyer, generally at delivery, a gap-filling rule the parties remain free to displace by contract but which otherwise resolves a question the CISG itself leaves open for domestic law to settle in a purely Swedish sale.",
