          "Now, turn to a further check on legislation, this one operating before a bill is even enacted rather than after, unlike the judicial review covered in the previous concept. Sweden has no dedicated constitutional court like Germany's Bundesverfassungsgericht, instead, the Lagrådet, the Council on Legislation, composed mainly of senior justices from the Högsta domstolen and the Högsta förvaltningsdomstolen, the Supreme Administrative Court, reviews draft legislation referred to it, generally bills touching constitutional rights, criminal law, or otherwise significant matters, before enactment for consistency with the constitution and general legal coherence, but its opinions are advisory only, not legally binding on the Riksdag, and the government is not strictly obliged to refer every bill to Lagrådet in the first place, though a failure to refer a genuinely significant bill is itself a point of real political and legal criticism. Lagrådet members serve on a part-time, rotating basis while remaining sitting justices of their home courts, and the council issues a written yttrande, opinion, on each referred bill, which is then published alongside the government's own proposition and becomes part of the bill's official förarbeten, preparatory works, that Swedish courts, as covered earlier in this lecture, treat as genuinely authoritative interpretive material, so a Lagrådet opinion can shape how a statute is actually read for decades even where the Riksdag simply ignored its substantive recommendation at the time.",
